WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.840
Redis is a program that keeps data in memory and answers requests over the network.

2
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.247
It keeps each piece of data under a name, called a key.

3
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:18.373
Here's one key: a ranked list of shops, 128 of them, in 1,305 bytes.

4
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:19.954
Add one more shop.

5
00:00:19.954 --> 00:00:21.531
Now it takes 10,813.

6
00:00:21.531 --> 00:00:24.530
One more item, eight times the memory.

7
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:28.396
Where do the extra nine thousand bytes come from?

8
00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:29.895
Hold that question.

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.042
Start with something familiar.

10
00:00:32.042 --> 00:00:42.529
Java's HashMap stores three fields of a payment like this: a table of slots, an entry for each field, and a separate piece of memory for each name and each value.

11
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.269
Pointers join them, 8-byte addresses that say where the next piece lives.

12
00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:49.711
And every piece carries a small header.

13
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:56.388
The real data here, status approved, amount 1250, currency EUR, is 35 bytes.

14
00:00:56.388 --> 00:01:00.125
On the lab server, the whole thing took 215.

15
00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:03.692
The bookkeeping weighs more than the data.

16
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:08.416
Multiply by ten million payments, and much of your memory holds pointers.

17
00:01:08.416 --> 00:01:10.472
What if we dropped the structure?

18
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:15.101
Picture a café owner who keeps her regulars on one notebook page.

19
00:01:15.101 --> 00:01:21.169
With a hundred names, the page is perfect: one sheet, and a finger finds anyone in seconds.

20
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:22.635
Redis does the same.

21
00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:31.577
It writes the entries one after another into one block of memory, each with its length in front, and finds a field by reading from the start.

22
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:34.477
That block is called a listpack.

23
00:01:34.477 --> 00:01:40.757
The same three fields take 77 bytes as a listpack, against 215 as a hash table.

24
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.859
Now the ranked list of shops.

25
00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:48.629
Redis calls it a sorted set: each member has a score, and the members stay in score order.

26
00:01:49.233 --> 00:01:52.248
Three shops: a listpack of 68 bytes.

27
00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:54.523
Add shop m-51 with score 41.

28
00:01:54.523 --> 00:02:02.528
Redis walks the block from the start until it passes 40, then moves every byte after that point to make room.

29
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:04.207
That walk sounds slow.

30
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:09.765
But a short block sits side by side in memory, and the CPU streams through it fast.

31
00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:18.912
After 124 more shops, the key holds 128 members: still one block, 1,305 bytes.

32
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.929
Where does the page break?

33
00:02:20.929 --> 00:02:21.461
At size.

34
00:02:21.461 --> 00:02:27.913
Walking ten thousand entries on every lookup, on the one thread every client shares, is too slow.

35
00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:31.771
And adding in the middle means moving every byte after it.

36
00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:39.532
So at a few thousand names, the owner switches to a box of index cards and a ranked list on the wall.

37
00:02:39.532 --> 00:02:40.956
Add the 129th shop.

38
00:02:40.956 --> 00:02:42.379
What does Redis do?

39
00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:43.577
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:54.508
Inside that one ZADD, Redis copies all 129 members into two new structures, and frees the block.

41
00:02:54.667 --> 00:03:00.945
A hash table maps each member to its score, like the box of cards: any shop's score in one step.

42
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:06.206
And a skip list keeps the members in score order, like the ranked list on the wall.

43
00:03:06.206 --> 00:03:11.548
It's a chain where some items carry extra links that jump far ahead, like express lanes.

44
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.975
Find shop m-51, score 41.

45
00:03:13.975 --> 00:03:18.238
Ride the top lane: 42 is too far, so drop a lane.

46
00:03:18.238 --> 00:03:20.239
40 is fine, move right.

47
00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:23.284
42 again is too far, so drop again.

48
00:03:23.284 --> 00:03:24.676
And there it is.

49
00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:30.176
Two structures, pointers everywhere, holding the same 129 members.

50
00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:33.783
That's where the bytes went: 10,813 of them.

51
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:35.665
The type didn't change.

52
00:03:35.665 --> 00:03:38.098
TYPE still says zset, a sorted set.

53
00:03:38.098 --> 00:03:44.284
What changed is the encoding, the layout behind the type, and OBJECT ENCODING reports it.

54
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:51.840
A sorted set switches past 128 members, or when any one member is longer than 64 bytes.

55
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.540
A hash switches past 512 fields.

56
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.263
Now remove the 129th shop.

57
00:03:57.263 --> 00:03:58.400
Back to 128.

58
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.241
Does it go back to a listpack?

59
00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:01.657
No.

60
00:04:01.657 --> 00:04:04.490
It stays a skip list, at 10,741 bytes.

61
00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.950
Here the notebook picture breaks.

62
00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.125
The owner can go back to one page when her list shrinks.

63
00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:14.629
Redis keeps the card box forever, just in case.

64
00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:17.677
To get the small form back, you rebuild the key.

65
00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:23.627
So if you'll have millions of a key, keep each one under the limits: few fields, short members.

66
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:30.292
The moral of the story: in memory, the bookkeeping around small items often weighs more than the items.

67
00:04:30.292 --> 00:04:36.625
Redis packs a small key into one block, and pays for the big structure only when the key outgrows it.

68
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.166
One quick check.

69
00:04:38.166 --> 00:04:41.094
A payment's hash grows to 600 fields.

70
00:04:41.094 --> 00:04:42.914
Listpack or hash table?

71
00:04:42.914 --> 00:04:44.180
Pause and think.

72
00:04:47.433 --> 00:04:48.383
Hash table.

73
00:04:48.383 --> 00:04:53.825
600 is past the hash limit of 512, and it won't come back down.
